  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{330}^{∘}).$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{330}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n-{330}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={30}^{∘}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{330}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}.$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254214/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${30}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{330}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$",
